# tests/T14.bd - T14: THE CHARGING UNIT.
#
# ONE QUESTION: is tick cost charged per PHYSICAL SOURCE LINE, per
# STATEMENT, or per EVALUATED OPERATION - and do comment lines, blank
# lines and `pass` consume budget when they sit inside an executed block?
#
# WHY IT MATTERS
#   editor_documentation.txt says flatly "Scripts run line by line. Each
#   line costs one tick." Against that, the v1.1.0 changelog admits a bug
#   where "the same function call, wrapped in identical arithmetic, could
#   cost wildly different amounts of tick budget depending only on which
#   side of the expression it sat on" - impossible unless the accounting
#   walks the expression tree. Both statements are still live.
#   The practical stake is the import phase. field_notes.txt asserts
#   import cost is "proportional to the imported file's line count (not
#   just its top-level statements - the whole file, function bodies
#   included)". utils/*.bd is 2,036 RAW lines against 945 executable ones.
#   If comments and blanks are free, that claim is wrong by 2.2x and every
#   import-debt number in field_notes is inflated by the codebase's own
#   documentation. If they are charged, this project pays double on every
#   import for its comments, and stripping them is a real, free fix.
#
# WHAT THIS TEST DOES THAT ITS NEIGHBOURS DO NOT
#   T05 (T05.bd) varies operations at a FIXED line count.
#   This one varies LINE COUNT at fixed operations - the exact complement.
#   Neither can name the unit alone; together they close it.
#
# THE DESIGN - nine arms, identical scaffolding, only the payload differs.
# The payload sits alone inside a get_unix_time() bracket; every sleep and
# every log is OUTSIDE that bracket, so instrumentation never enters the
# measurement and cancels exactly in arm-to-arm differences.
#
#   arm      physical  stmts  adds  calls   what it isolates
#   BASE        0        0      0     0     zero point
#   LINE24     24       24     24     0     \ same 24 additions,
#   OPS24       2        2     24     0     / 12x different line count
#   CALL        3        3     24     3     \ same 3 calls, same 8 args,
#   WRAP       24        3     24     3     / only the newlines differ
#   CMNT       25        1      1     0     24 comment lines + 1 sentinel
#   BLNK       25        1      1     0     24 blank lines + 1 sentinel
#   PASS       25       25      1     0     24 `pass` + 1 sentinel
#   BASEB       0        0      0     0     drift control, must match BASE
#
#   LINE24 / OPS24 / CALL / WRAP each add EXACTLY 24 to `a` per round.
#   Four different source shapes, byte-identical arithmetic result. That is
#   the integrity guarantee: if a= steps by 24 in all four, they really did
#   the same work and only the layout differed.
#
# HOW TO READ IT  (U = sec_per_unit from T02/T02.bd;
#                  U ~ 0.10s at base tier. Deltas are per-round block=,
#                  always against the SAME run's BASE, never another run.)
#
#   HEADLINE 1 - the ratio  R = (OPS24 - BASE) / (LINE24 - BASE)
#     R ~ 0.08  -> cost tracks LINES or STATEMENTS, not arithmetic.
#     R ~ 0.5   -> cost tracks expression NODES (leaves counted too).
#     R ~ 1.0   -> cost tracks OPERATORS - 24 adds cost 24 either way.
#     R > 1.5   -> operators cost MORE than the lines holding them; the
#                  "each line costs one tick" doc line is simply false.
#
#   HEADLINE 2 - the difference  D = WRAP - CALL
#     D ~ +21U (+2.1s at base tier) -> RAW PHYSICAL lines are charged:
#         a call split over 8 lines costs 8. Wrapping long calls for
#         readability is silently expensive, everywhere in this codebase.
#     D ~ 0    -> continuation lines are free; the unit is at least a
#         logical line / statement.
#
#   HEADLINE 3 - the dead lines  (subtract 1U for each arm's sentinel)
#     CMNT - BASE - U ~ +24U -> comments are charged. Then the unit is the
#         RAW LINE and nothing else, because a comment has zero statements
#         and zero operations. field_notes' 2,036-line import claim stands,
#         and deleting comments from utils/*.bd is an immediate real fix.
#     CMNT - BASE - U ~ 0    -> comments are free (stripped at tokenise).
#         The import-cost model must be re-based on 945 executable lines.
#     BLNK behaves like CMNT unless the tokeniser treats them differently -
#         a split verdict (one free, one charged) is a genuine finding.
#     PASS - BASE - U ~ +24U -> `pass` is a real charged statement.
#     PASS - BASE - U ~ 0    -> `pass` is compiled out.
#
#   THE COMBINATION IS THE ANSWER:
#     CMNT charged                      -> PER RAW PHYSICAL LINE
#     CMNT free, PASS charged, R ~ 0.08 -> PER STATEMENT
#     CMNT free, PASS free,    R >= 0.5 -> PER EVALUATED OPERATION
#     Any other combination is new and must be reported as-is, not forced
#     into one of these three boxes.
#
#   INVALID RUN: if BASE is not the smallest mean block=, or if BASEB
#   differs from BASE by more than the spread WITHIN either phase, the run
#   drifted - report it and re-run. Late arms (CMNT/BLNK/PASS) should then
#   be read against BASEB, which sits next to them, not against BASE.
#
#   INTEGER CLOCK: if every block= prints as a whole number, get_unix_time()
#   has 1-second resolution. Then IGNORE block= and read sum= instead: it
#   aggregates 3 rounds, so every prediction above triples (24U per round
#   becomes 7.2s per phase) and survives the quantisation.
#
#   INTEGRITY - a= at each phase end must read exactly:
#     BASE 0 / LINE24 72 / OPS24 144 / CALL 216 / WRAP 288 /
#     CMNT 291 / BLNK 294 / PASS 297 / BASEB 297.
#   BLOCK TRUNCATION: if a dead line ENDS the loop block, that phase prints
#   exactly ONE round line instead of three, with round=3 and a= up only 1.
#   That is a real language finding - report it - and that phase's block=
#   must be discarded. `r` is incremented FIRST inside every loop precisely
#   so this failure cannot hang the script.
#
# SAFETY - THE PAYLOADS ARE DELIBERATE UNPACED BURSTS; that is the
# measurement. Every one is sized to stay inside the ~5s-at-100% kill:
# worst case 25 charged lines + the bracket line = ~2.6s at base tier,
# with sleep(2) before and sleep(1) after every single one. No get_cpu()
# anywhere in this file.
#   GATE - DO NOT RUN THIS UNTIL T02 HAS REPORTED. This file's burst sizes
#   are calibrated against base tier (24 charged lines ~ 2.4s, half the
#   kill window). If T02 measured sec_per_unit > 0.16 s/unit, or if T02's
#   30-unit burst KILLED the script, do NOT run this as written and do NOT
#   hand-trim it in-game: every a= value below is computed for 24-unit
#   payloads and a hand-trim silently voids the integrity check. Report
#   T02's number back instead and take a re-cut 12-unit version of this
#   file, with its own a= table.
#
# HOW TO RUN
#   1) Run `ps` and `jobs` first, confirm nothing else is running, paste
#      that too.
#   2) Save as ~/scripts/T14.bd. BEFORE RUNNING, open it in
#      the editor and confirm the file is 434 lines long (37 of those are
#      blank: 24 in PHASE-BLNK, 13 between phases). If it is short by
#      ~24 or ~37, the PHASE-BLNK blank lines were eaten by the paste - blanks
#      that silently vanish look exactly like blanks that are free, which
#      is the one false negative this test cannot detect on its own. Fix
#      the paste before running.
#   3) Run `T14`. Paste back EVERY line printed, verbatim.
#   4) Run it TWICE, back to back, same session.
#   5) A third run only if the two disagree on a verdict, not merely on
#      the numbers.
#   RECOVERY - a parse error means NOTHING runs, so read the error first:
#     - error naming the multi-line call -> v1.1.1's "function calls can
#       span multiple lines" is FALSE. Paste it verbatim (that is a
#       headline result), delete the whole PHASE-WRAP section, re-run.
#       Every a= printed after that point drops by 72; the per-round
#       steps, which is what integrity rests on, are unchanged.
#     - error naming `pass` -> paste it verbatim (that answers the pass
#       question outright: `pass` is documented in editor_documentation.txt
#       but may not be implemented), delete the whole PHASE-PASS section,
#       re-run. a= then ends at 294, not 297.
#   ~2 to 2.5 minutes per run at base tier.

var t0 = get_unix_time()
var a = 0
var r = 0
var ts = 0
var te = 0
var bl = 0
var acc = 0
var m0 = 0
var span = 0

func s8(p1, p2, p3, p4, p5, p6, p7, p8):
    return p1 + p2 + p3 + p4 + p5 + p6 + p7 + p8

log("T14-CLOCK raw=" + str(t0) + " (decimals => float clock, read block=; whole number => 1s clock, read sum=)")
log("T14-START arms=BASE,LINE24,OPS24,CALL,WRAP,CMNT,BLNK,PASS,BASEB rounds=3 get_cpu_calls=0 a_final_must_be=297")
sleep(2)

log("T14-BEGIN arm=BASE shape=0lines_0adds_0calls")
m0 = get_unix_time()
r = 0
acc = 0
while r < 3:
    r = r + 1
    sleep(2)
    ts = get_unix_time()
    te = get_unix_time()
    sleep(1)
    bl = te - ts
    acc = acc + bl
    log("T14 arm=BASE round=" + str(r) + " block=" + str(bl) + " a=" + str(a) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
span = get_unix_time() - m0
log("T14-END arm=BASE rounds=" + str(r) + " sum=" + str(acc) + " span=" + str(span) + " a=" + str(a) + " (a must be 0)")

log("T14-BEGIN arm=LINE24 shape=24lines_24adds_0calls")
m0 = get_unix_time()
r = 0
acc = 0
while r < 3:
